+This paper developed a framework for making context-dependent meaning legible within a polysemic symbolic field, moving from the contextual salience of individual descriptors and symbols to the reconfiguration of the relations among them. Inspired by the Lakota Shape Kit, the framework showed that different framings induce distinct and interpretable relational configurations, that these configurations evolve coherently as a narrative unfolds, and that their degree of concentration indicates whether a context favors a dominant reading or sustains several symbols at once. At the level of whole contexts, calibrated recoupling fingerprints provided a more field-specific basis for comparing, clustering, and retrieving passages according to how they engage the symbolic system. The same framework extended from text to sound, allowing recordings to probe the field directly and ambiguous acoustics to surface latent connections across it. This work offers a relational framework for making context-dependent meaning in symbolic systems more legible, with the $\Delta$ coupling matrix as its central interpretive instrument.
+
+Read as a whole, the framework supplies a legible, relational, and community-grounded readout of how a language model's representation relates contexts to a curated symbolic system, and it sits at the meeting point of three programs. For computational linguistics and interpretability, it turns the opaque geometry of context--descriptor similarity into a per-relation pattern in a designed coordinate system, complementing point-level probes with a view of the relations themselves. For cross-cultural knowledge representation, it lets an Indigenous symbolic tradition serve as the very coordinate system through which a model is read, so the analysis speaks in the categories that matter to the community. For creative AI, it exposes the ambiguity and relational pathways of the symbolic system as control surfaces an artist can navigate.